---
/* GENERATED from suite.json (roadmap 147.1). A hand-written list of 28 screens
   would be a second description of the suite, free to drift from it — the same
   reason /patterns/ is built from patterns.json and /concepts/which-pattern
   refuses to be a fifth interpretation of the pattern system.

   This page exists because the suite was INVISIBLE. It has been built and
   gated on every commit since Slice 130 — 28 screens, six modules, zero CSS of
   its own, axe-clean at two widths — and nothing in the docs linked to it. A
   person evaluating this framework could read 39 pattern pages and never see a
   finished screen. */
import Gallery from '../../layouts/Gallery.astro';
import Related from '../../components/Related.astro';
import suite from '../../data/suite.json';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const kinds = [...new Set(suite.modules.flatMap((m: any) => m.screens.map((s: any) => s.kind)))];
---
<Gallery title="Screen kit">
  <p class="demo-note">
    <strong>{suite.total} complete ERP screens</strong>, across {suite.modules.length}
    modules, built from this framework and <em>nothing else</em> — no CSS of
    their own, not one line. Open one, read its markup, take what you need.
    They are the answer to "yes, but what does a real screen look like?".
    <strong>Not a component gallery</strong> — if you want one control and its
    settings, <a href={base + '/components/button'}>the component pages</a> are
    that. This is whole screens, with realistic data, in the shape an ERP
    actually uses them.
  </p>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Why these exist, and what that buys you</h2>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">The suite is not a showcase — it is this
    project's <strong>gap-finding instrument</strong>. Every screen is built
    only from shipped CSS, and a build gate fails if any of them adds a single
    declaration of its own. When a screen cannot be built, that is a hole in the
    framework and it gets recorded and usually fixed. Twenty-one gaps have been
    found this way.</p>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">Which is the useful part for you: <strong>these
    screens are proof the framework covers them.</strong> They are rebuilt and
    re-audited on every commit — axe at 1440 and 390, no sideways scroll, every
    data table named, every internal link resolving. If a screen here looks
    right, the CSS behind it is the CSS you install.</p>
  </section>

  {suite.modules.map((m: any) => (
    <section class="demo">
      <h2>{m.label}</h2>
      <div class="bo-data-table-container" tabindex="0" data-density="compact">
        <table class="bo-data-table">
          <caption class="bo-visually-hidden">{m.label} screens, what each one is for</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Screen</th>
              <th scope="col">Kind</th>
              <th scope="col">What it is for</th>
              <th scope="col">Markup</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {m.screens.map((s: any) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">
                  <a class="bo-data-table__cell-link" href={base + s.href}>{s.title}</a>
                </th>
                <td><span class="bo-badge bo-badge--type">{s.kind}</span></td>
                <td>{s.blurb}</td>
                <td><a href={base + s.markup}>copy</a></td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>
  ))}

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>How to take one</h2>
    <ol class="bo-stack bo-stack--tight docs-list">
      <li><strong>Open the screen</strong> and use it. Sort, filter, select rows,
      open a drawer — it is a real page, not a picture of one.</li>
      <li><strong>Take the markup.</strong> The <em>copy</em> link beside each
      screen serves that screen's <code>&lt;main&gt;</code> as plain text —
      no shell, no document wrapper, nothing to unpick. It is cut from the
      unprefixed build, so the links in it are ordinary app paths rather than
      this site's.</li>
      <li><strong>Install the CSS</strong> —
      <a href={base + '/getting-started/installation'}>installation</a> is one
      import — and paste. The classes resolve because they are the same ones
      this site ships.</li>
      <li><strong>Check your markup</strong> with
      <code>npx bo-check-markup dist</code>, the same gate the suite passes: it
      fails on a class or a <code>data-*</code> value the framework does not
      define. A typo becomes a build error rather than a silently dead rule.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">Screens are kinds — {kinds.join(', ')} — and the
    kind is what a screen owes. A list owes a way to narrow the set and a way to
    act on several at once; a report owes a total and a way to take it away.
    That is enforced, not aspirational:
    <a href={base + '/concepts/which-pattern'}>which pattern should I use?</a>
    walks the same decision from the other end.</p>
  </section>

  <Related links={[
    ['/getting-started/installation', 'Installation'],
    ['/getting-started/first-screen', 'Build your first screen'],
    ['/patterns/', 'All patterns'],
    ['/concepts/which-pattern', 'Which pattern should I use?'],
  ]} />
</Gallery>
